nd use the first time they read it.",["Plain Writing Act of 2010"]],["p-27aa2317c700d041.html","source-block-313","Normative links",["Plain Writing Act of 2010"]],["p-27aa2317c700d041.html","source-block-335","Law: https://www.govinfo.gov/app/details/PLAW-111publ274",["Plain Writing Act of 2010","Normative links"]],["p-27aa2317c700d041.html","source-block-396","Federal plain-language guidance: https://www.plainlanguage.gov/",["Plain Writing Act of 2010","Normative links"]],["p-27aa2317c700d041.html","source-block-464","Scope",["Plain Writing Act of 2010"]],["p-27aa2317c700d041.html","source-block-476","Covers documents federal agencies issue to the public. The federal plain-language guidelines extend the law into concrete writing practice.",["Plain Writing Act of 2010","Scope"]],["p-27aa2317c700d041.html","source-block-620","Maintainer",["Plain Writing Act of 2010"]],["p-27aa2317c700d041.html","source-block-637","Guidance is maintained by PLAIN (Plain Language Action and Information Network) at plainlanguage.gov.",["Plain Writing Act of 2010","Maintainer"]],["p-0cfada32c999ea4d.html","source-block-0","Usability",[]],["p-0cfada32c999ea4d.html","source-block-12","via Usability - Glossary | CSRC",["Usability"]],["p-0cfada32c999ea4d.html","source-block-140","Per ISO/IEC 9241-11: Extent to which a product can be used by specified users to achieve specified goals with effectiveness, efficiency, and satisfaction in a specified context of use.",["Usability","via Usability - Glossary | CSRC"]],["p-0cfada32c999ea4d.html","source-block-330","Sources:",["Usability","via Usability - Glossary | CSRC","Per ISO/IEC 9241-11: Extent to which a product can be used by specified users to achieve specified goals with effectiveness, efficiency, and satisfaction in a s"]],["p-0cfada32c999ea4d.html","source-block-349","NIST SP 800-63-3 from ISO/IEC 9241-11",["via Usability - Glossary | CSRC","Per ISO/IEC 9241-11: Extent to which a product can be used by specified users to achieve specified goals with effectiveness, efficiency, and satisfaction in a s","Sources:"]],["p-0cfada32c999ea4d.html","source-block-440","Extent to which a product can be used by specified users to achieve specified goals with effectiveness, efficiency, and satisfaction in a specified context of use.",["Usability","via Usability - Glossary | CSRC"]],["p-0cfada32c999ea4d.html","source-block-609","Sources:",["Usability","via Usability - Glossary | CSRC","Extent to which a product can be used by specified users to achieve specified goals with effectiveness, efficiency, and satisfaction in a specified context of u"]],["p-0cfada32c999ea4d.html","source-block-628","NIST SP 800-63-3 from ISO/IEC 9241-11",["via Usability - Glossary | CSRC","Extent to which a product can be used by specified users to achieve specified goals with effectiveness, efficiency, and satisfaction in a specified context of u","Sources:"]],["p-0cfada32c999ea4d.html","source-block-719","The extent to which a product can be used by specified users to achieve specified goals with effectiveness, efficiency and satisfaction in a specified context of use.",["Usability","via Usability - Glossary | CSRC"]],["p-0cfada32c999ea4d.html","source-block-891","Sources:",["Usability","via Usability - Glossary | CSRC","The extent to which a product can be used by specified users to achieve specified goals with effectiveness, efficiency and satisfaction in a specified context o"]],["p-0cfada32c999ea4d.html","source-block-910","NISTIR 8040 from ISO 9241-11:1998",["via Usability - Glossary | CSRC","The extent to which a product can be used by specified users to achieve specified goals with effectiveness, efficiency and satisfaction in a specified context o","Sources:"]],["p-1e84e2a7b597742a.html","source-block-0","Usage",[]],["p-1e84e2a7b597742a.html","source-block-34","Usage Specification | Usage",["Usage"]],["p-3894d9a18a2d59d8.html","source-block-0","USB Host Mode vs Client Mode for Virtual Ethernet over USB Conceptual Overview",[]],["p-3894d9a18a2d59d8.html","source-block-83","Overview",["USB Host Mode vs Client Mode for Virtual Ethernet over USB Conceptual Overview"]],["p-3894d9a18a2d59d8.html","source-block-98","USB links are asymmetric by design: one side is the , and the other side is the (also called peripheral or device mode).",["USB Host Mode vs Client Mode for Virtual Ethernet over USB Conceptual Overview","Overview"]],["p-3894d9a18a2d59d8.html","source-block-242","This asymmetry is why a USB cable is not automatically a \"network cable.\"",["USB Host Mode vs Client Mode for Virtual Ethernet over USB Conceptual Overview","Overview"]],["p-3894d9a18a2d59d8.html","source-block-320","A USB cable can carry virtual Ethernet only when protocol roles and software support align on both ends.",["USB Host Mode vs Client Mode for Virtual Ethernet over USB Conceptual Overview","Overview"]],["p-3894d9a18a2d59d8.html","source-block-429","Context",["USB Host Mode vs Client Mode for Virtual Ethernet over USB Conceptual Overview"]],["p-3894d9a18a2d59d8.html","source-block-443","People often expect two computers connected by USB to behave like two computers connected by Ethernet.",["USB Host Mode vs Client Mode for Virtual Ethernet over USB Conceptual Overview","Context"]],["p-3894d9a18a2d59d8.html","source-block-550","Ethernet is peer-to-peer at the cable level, but USB is controller-to-device at the bus level.",["USB Host Mode vs Client Mode for Virtual Ethernet over USB Conceptual Overview","Context"]],["p-3894d9a18a2d59d8.html","source-block-649","The misunderstanding usually comes from focusing on the connector shape (for example, USB/Connector/Ref/USB-C) instead of the protocol roles.",["USB Host Mode vs Client Mode for Virtual Ethernet over USB Conceptual Overview","Context"]],["p-3894d9a18a2d59d8.html","source-block-799","Key Principles",["USB Host Mode vs Client Mode for Virtual Ethernet over USB Conceptual Overview"]],["p-3894d9a18a2d59d8.html","source-block-820","Single-bus control",["USB Host Mode vs Client Mode for Virtual Ethernet over USB Conceptual Overview","Key Principles"]],["p-3894d9a18a2d59d8.html","source-block-847","The host owns bus scheduling, enumeration, and power policy.",["USB Host Mode vs Client Mode for Virtual Ethernet over USB Conceptual Overview","Key Principles","Single-bus control"]],["p-3894d9a18a2d59d8.html","source-block-913","The client responds to host requests and exposes functions.",["USB Host Mode vs Client Mode for Virtual Ethernet over USB Conceptual Overview","Key Principles","Single-bus control"]],["p-3894d9a18a2d59d8.html","source-block-978","Role is a protocol state, not just a plug shape",["USB Host Mode vs Client Mode for Virtual Ethernet over USB Conceptual Overview","Key Principles"]],["p-3894d9a18a2d59d8.html","source-block-1034","USB-C connectors are physically reversible, but role assignment still must resolve to host/client behavior.",["USB Host Mode vs Client Mode for Virtual Ethernet over USB Conceptual Overview","Key Principles","Role is a protocol state, not just a plug shape"]],["p-3894d9a18a2d59d8.html","source-block-1147","Dual-role-capable hardware can switch roles, but both ends cannot behave as host on the same USB link.",["USB Host Mode vs Client Mode for Virtual Ethernet over USB Conceptual Overview","Key Principles","Role is a protocol state, not just a plug shape"]],["p-3894d9a18a2d59d8.html","source-block-1255","Cable capability is necessary but not sufficient",["USB Host Mode vs Client Mode for Virtual Ethernet over USB Conceptual Overview","Key Principles"]],["p-3894d9a18a2d59d8.html","source-block-1312","A cable may carry only power, or power plus limited data lanes.",["USB Host Mode vs Client Mode for Virtual Ethernet over USB Conceptual Overview","Key Principles","Cable capability is necessary but not sufficient"]],["p-3894d9a18a2d59d8.html","source-block-1381","Even with a full data-capable cable, virtual Ethernet still requires compatible USB networking functions and drivers at both endpoints.",["USB Host Mode vs Client Mode for Virtual Ethernet over USB Conceptual Overview","Key Principles","Cable capability is necessary but not sufficient"]],["p-3894d9a18a2d59d8.html","source-block-1522","Mechanism",["USB Host Mode vs Client Mode for Virtual Ethernet over USB Conceptual Overview"]],["p-3894d9a18a2d59d8.html","source-block-1538","For virtual Ethernet over USB, one side presents a USB network device function (for example ECM, NCM, or RNDIS-class behavior), and the other side runs the matching host-side driver.",["USB Host Mode vs Client Mode for Virtual Ethernet over USB Conceptual Overview","Mechanism"]],["p-3894d9a18a2d59d8.html","source-block-1725","The host enumerates the device and binds a network interface.",["USB Host Mode vs Client Mode for Virtual Ethernet over USB Conceptual Overview","Mechanism"]],["p-3894d9a18a2d59d8.html","source-block-1791","The operating system then treats that interface like a NIC, but transport is still USB transactions underneath.",["USB Host Mode vs Client Mode for Virtual Ethernet over USB Conceptual Overview","Mechanism"]],["p-3894d9a18a2d59d8.html","source-block-1907","If both connected systems only expose host mode on that port, enumeration does not complete as a host/client pair, so no USB network interface appears.",["USB Host Mode vs Client Mode for Virtual Ethernet over USB Conceptual Overview","Mechanism"]],["p-3894d9a18a2d59d8.html","source-block-2063","Examples",["USB Host Mode vs Client Mode for Virtual Ethernet over USB Conceptual Overview"]],["p-3894d9a18a2d59d8.html","source-block-2078","Works",["USB Host Mode vs Client Mode for Virtual Ethernet over USB Conceptual Overview","Examples"]],["p-3894d9a18a2d59d8.html","source-block-2092","A laptop (host) connected to an embedded board in USB device/gadget mode that exposes a USB Ethernet function.",["USB Host Mode vs Client Mode for Virtual Ethernet over USB Conceptual Overview","Examples","Works"]],["p-3894d9a18a2d59d8.html","source-block-2208","Usually does not work directly",["USB Host Mode vs Client Mode for Virtual Ethernet over USB Conceptual Overview","Examples"]],["p-3894d9a18a2d59d8.html","source-block-2247","Two laptops connected with an ordinary USB-C cable when neither side exposes a compatible USB device-mode network function for that link.",["USB Host Mode vs Client Mode for Virtual Ethernet over USB Conceptual Overview","Examples","Usually does not work directly"]],["p-3894d9a18a2d59d8.html","source-block-2390","Role-dependent",["USB Host Mode vs Client Mode for Virtual Ethernet over USB Conceptual Overview","Examples"]],["p-3894d9a18a2d59d8.html","source-block-2413","Two dual-role USB-C systems may work only if one side successfully enters device mode and both OS stacks support a common USB networking protocol.",["USB Host Mode vs Client Mode for Virtual Ethernet over USB Conceptual Overview","Examples","Role-dependent"]],["p-3894d9a18a2d59d8.html","source-block-2565","Misconceptions",["USB Host Mode vs Client Mode for Virtual Ethernet over USB Conceptual Overview"]],["p-3894d9a18a2d59d8.html","source-block-2586","\"Any USB cable can be used as an Ethernet cable\" -> False. The cable alone cannot create peer networking semantics.",["USB Host Mode vs Client Mode for Virtual Ethernet over USB Conceptual Overview","Misconceptions"]],["p-3894d9a18a2d59d8.html","source-block-2710","\"If the connector is USB-C, networking should just work\" -> False. USB-C defines connector and negotiation mechanisms, not guaranteed network-function support.",["USB Host Mode vs Client Mode for Virtual Ethernet over USB Conceptual Overview","Misconceptions"]],["p-3894d9a18a2d59d8.html","source-block-2878","\"Power delivery implies data networking support\" -> False. Many cables and port modes negotiate power without exposing the needed data/function path.",["USB Host Mode vs Client Mode for Virtual Ethernet over USB Conceptual Overview","Misconceptions"]],["p-3894d9a18a2d59d8.html","source-block-3036","Related",["USB Host Mode vs Client Mode for Virtual Ethernet over USB Conceptual Overview"]],["p-3894d9a18a2d59d8.html","source-block-3050","USB/Connector/Ref/USB-C",["USB Host Mode vs Client Mode for Virtual Ethernet over USB Conceptual Overview","Related"]],["p-3894d9a18a2d59d8.html","source-block-3082","USB/Connector/Ref/USB-A",["USB Host Mode vs Client Mode for Virtual Ethernet over USB Conceptual Overview","Related"]],["p-3894d9a18a2d59d8.html","source-block-3114","USB/Connector/Ref/USB-B",["USB Host Mode vs Client Mode for Virtual Ethernet over USB Conceptual Overview","Related"]],["p-09b0115d00faa1cc.html","source-block-59","USB Connector Reference Index",[]],["p-09b0115d00faa1cc.html","source-block-93","Visual Reference",["USB Connector Reference Index"]],["p-09b0115d00faa1cc.html","source-block-276","Photo: public domain, via Wikimedia Commons",["USB Connector Reference Index","Visual Reference"]],["p-09b0115d00faa1cc.html","source-block-383","Overview",["USB Connector Reference Index"]],["p-09b0115d00faa1cc.html","source-block-398","Quick reference guide to USB connector types documented in this knowledge base",["USB Connector Reference Index","Overview"]],["p-09b0115d00faa1cc.html","source-block-481","Provides comparison of key specifications and links to detailed connector pages",["USB Connector Reference Index","Overview"]],["p-09b0115d00faa1cc.html","source-block-565","Organized by connector family and generation",["USB Connector Reference Index","Overview"]],["p-09b0115d00faa1cc.html","source-block-614","Connector Comparison Table",["USB Connector Reference Index"]],["p-09b0115d00faa1cc.html","source-block-647","Standard Connectors",["USB Connector Reference Index","Connector Comparison Table"]],["p-09b0115d00faa1cc.html","source-block-1239","Mini/Micro Connectors",["USB Connector Reference Index","Connector Comparison Table"]],["p-09b0115d00faa1cc.html","source-block-1691","Proprietary Connectors",["USB Connector Reference Index","Connector Comparison Table"]],["p-09